=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Router6 acts as the default gateway for two networks: 172.18.50.0/24 (laptop4/5 on switch11) and 172.18.51.0/24 (pc15–18 on switch10), connecting them to the broader campus via eth2 (10.50.1.2/24 → router3). Its iptables FORWARD chain has three DROP rules applied specifically to traffic arriving inbound on eth2: rule 2 drops all TCP, rule 3 drops all UDP, and rule 4 drops all ICMP. The only exception is rule 1, which accepts TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1. This makes the mswitch2-side networks reachable from the outside only via that single narrow exception.

Laptop0 sits on 172.16.20.0/24 (mswitch1/router0 side), which matches none of the exceptions. Every packet laptop0 sends toward 172.18.50.x or 172.18.51.x arrives at router6's eth2 inbound and is immediately dropped — ICMP by rule 4 (3 packets / 252 bytes already counted), TCP by rule 2, UDP by rule 3. Routing is not the problem: router6 has valid RIP-learned routes for 172.16.20.0/24 and the path through router3 is intact. The mswitch2-side hosts work fine among themselves because intra-group traffic only traverses eth0↔eth1, never entering eth2.

Root cause: an overly restrictive iptables FORWARD policy on router6's eth2 interface blocks all inter-group traffic, effectively banning laptop0's entire subnet.

Fix: remove rules 2–4 from the FORWARD chain on router6 (`iptables -D FORWARD` in reverse order — 4, 3, 2). If a stateful firewall boundary is intended, replace them with `-m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT` before the DROP rules so return traffic is permitted.
=== END ===